ee in a little bit that's all  relative  the csf then braids the  bathes the entire brain in the csf  which allows the compound to be  distributed throughout the entire brain  what makes this rather uniquely  difficult as a surgical procedure  is you're effectively working blind  you're injecting  into an area of the body that  you can't see where you certainly can't  see it unless you're going to do a  tremendous amount  of operation and  potentially causing tremendous amount of  trauma  we can't see the area that we're going  to be  ultimately injecting in  so what we need to do is we use  landmarks in this case bregma which is  the intersection of two sutures on top  of the skull  and then an atlas which is designed to  tell you  in three dimensions where do you go from  that particular point  to find the spot that you're trying to  access  and as you can imagine properly  identifying bregma  is incredibly important especially when  you're aiming for a target  that is a tenth of a millimeter or  smaller  this is what it looks like in a live  mouse  plenty of undulations it is not nearly  as  easy to discern where the intersection  actually is as it appears in a nice line  drawing  these are indications of where we are  generally targeting  the black spaces on the right are those  lateral ventricles and you can see  in a euthanized animal where we've done  a dye injection  on the upper left you can see the dye in  the lateral ventricles  and then the lighter shading in the  picture below  is the csf area itself  and what makes this uniquely challenging  is targeting if you were to pull a  credit card or a debit card out of your  wallet  we're aiming for a target that is  approximately one-half to one-third  the depth of your card so turn it on end  look at it  we wish it were that large so  it's not necessarily the most  technically challenging  of procedures but it is very very  precise  and then finally to add a little bit of  a challenge  different strains of animals have  different head shapes  the atlases that we use are based off a  relatively small  number of animals in one very specific  strain also  males not females and there are  differences between the two  so there are a lot of things that we  have to do to make sure that in that  particular strain  of that particular group at that  particular age  that we're actually hitting what we want  to hit and i have other  ones out there on that so i'm not going  to spend a lot of time on that  because here's the point we met with a  client  at the tail end of last year a large  pharmaceutical  and they wanted us to develop this  resource in-house  running approximately one study per week  and so we had an original plan  somewhat aggressive but doable we were  going to obtain the equipment in january  and february  in march we were going to run a pilot  study  and start generating individual studies  for our clients  by april and may we would be bringing  the apprentice surgeons  so that we would have some depth to the  capability  may june we would be validating them and  all of this  until approximately february to march of  this year  seemed like a fairly straightforward  thing we've all in this industry done  this multiple times  but of course there was one minor  unexpected challenge  and that forced a lot of changes to us  and what i'd like to spend the bulk of  this time  discussing is what are the things that  came up  what were the things that we expected  and how are we able to overcome these  challenges  so that all of us as this pandemic  continues  affecting us how are we ready for the  next  unexpected obstacle if you will  and the challenges were many there were  challenges to staffing there were  challenges to supply chains  this requires very specialized equipment  with a handful of manufacturers  most of them seem to be based in either  new york state or california which of  course very aggressively closed down  we also run  anywhere from dozens to  over a hundred studies concurrently  within my group  and the exact same challenges were  hitting every single study  every single sponsor and client that we  had were dealing with the same things as  well so there were ripple effects that  affected us in here  communication face-to-face meetings went  away  the ability to sit in a meeting room  with the various stakeholders  disappeared  and measuring success or failure again  everything was distributed it made many  of our normal functions  remarkably more difficult  we were successful not to give you a  spoiler  here's why and this is really important  you need a solid foundation  that can roll with the various punches  that you're going to have  i have a very motivated and capable  technical staff i have outstanding  supervisors  i have a great management tree without  that  none of this would have been successful  because this was vastly harder  for my technicians and supervisors than  we would have expected  knowing the procedure helped and so the  fact that  i and a few others had a significant  amount of experience  really really helped because we were  able  because we understood the nuances and  the depths of the procedure  we were able to problem solve in a way  that had we been new to this  we probably would not have been able to  again i mentioned upper management  you need their support i had it if we  hadn't  i would have a very very different talk  for today  it would probably be mostly along the  lines of what not to do  during a pandemic  we needed resources beyond my group um  we have an outstanding study director  and nick jones we have  the people around us who were able to  contribute scientific  necropsy all kinds of  affiliated groups that were also all  dealing  with the same things and they had solid  and they had to be able to roll with  these punches as well  we're going to talk a lot about  relationships our vendors  saved us had we  not had vendors who were going to go  above and beyond  we wear surgical masks you all may  recall that back in march and april it  was remarkably hard to get those  lab coats disposable surgical gloves  sterile supplies suture  all of those things became much harder  to find and we were fortunate to have  vendors  who were able to anticipate  substitute work with us wouldn't have  been able to do it without them  and you need a communication  infrastructure you know we talk about  zoom or skype or team site or what have  you  all of those have to be in place  beforehand you have to be used to  working with them  because again if these hadn't been  in place we would have been in deep  trouble trying to develop them while  also dealing with all the other  difficulties  and this is going to keep coming at us  in waves  so we're taking the lessons that we've  learned we're moving on so we do  better and better the next time but we  also know that we're going to be testing  all of these functions  probably fairly frequently for some time  to come  so this is my personal favorite i've  been on several disaster planning  committees  over the years and what seems to be  fairly consistent is the disaster plan  is the thing that you spend a lot of  time in conference rooms discussing  you come up with a whole series of  suggestions  and an implementation strategy that's  going to take a significant amount of  work  that's going to take a significant  amount of money stockpiling supplies  ppe etc and in my experience what  usually  happens is you do a very watered down  version of it  because of course never have to actually  roll our disaster plan  of course we did again very fortunate  in that within the company that i am at  within  the site that i am at we did have a  disaster plan  we did follow it we were as ready as we  could be  um and one of the things i put out there  is  your disaster plan not only do you need  one it has to be something that you can  follow  you can come up with a fantastic  disaster plan i've certainly worked on  committees that have came up  with outstanding disaster plans that  were far too ambitious to follow  so we didn't do anything um it's much  better to have one  that is let's say a seven out of ten but  you're actually going to do it  than one that is a 10 out of 10 that  you're not going to  and again it has to be ready in advance  we had alternate suppliers identified  we had triggers for extra procurement we  had already started cross-training  and identifying personnel resources  throughout the site throughout the  company  in case we had to cover for people who  were no longer going to be on site for a  certain period of time  and we also had a prioritization scheme  so what do you do if  you simply can't stretch that blanket  far enough to cover  everything how do you maximize  your resources to get the critical work  done  and affect the on going  because we're responsible to our clients  they contracted with us we owe them  so how do we minimize the impact on them  when we're going through this as well  which again goes back to the scientific  expertise  that's a decision that has to be made  with several people providing input  and if you don't have them available  you're going to have a difficult time  it's people ultimately at the end of the  day  everything that we do to deal with this  revolves around people  there are employees there are the other  critical groups in-house  there are clients they're our suppliers  they're the local authorities because  you may need a nice letter that says  that you're essential personnel so that  you can go back and forth to work  we had those and as  i'm emphasizing and i can't emphasize it  enough  this is based on the relationships that  you have already established and if you  don't have them yet  you're never going to have a better time  than today to start  you need those in place you cannot be  reaching out to people who don't know  you  and asking them to assist you because  they're getting asked by  heaven knows how many people and if  those people  have developed that type of a personal  relationship  human beings are human beings who are  they going to help  first  so again you can change a relationship  but it's really really  hard when everything is going south and  everyone is under tremendous stress and  everyone is under tremendous stress  i bring up the people in my department  who again they're wonderful  but part of the reason that we were able  to weather this  is before this happened we pushed  responsibility  and decision making authority down the  organizational chart as much as we could  we gave them the training we were  initiative  and we gave them the authority to make  their own best decisions  you can't have a choke point and  certainly the larger the group you have  you can have a very top-down management  style that'll work  when times are normal when things are  constantly up in the air  and you as the leadership is getting  pulled  in a whole series of different  directions you never anticipated  if everyone is dependent upon you or a  very small group of people to make every  single decision  you will fail and i've watched it happen  before  i've watched it happen during this  pandemic um  and one of the pieces is not just you  trust them  do they trust you do they believe  and understand and do they have evidence  that  you're going to give them that ability  to make those decisions  and you're going to protect them because  we do make bad decisions i make bad  decisions  everybody does do they know that they  are  going to make the best decision possible  or is it a risk for them  because if it's a risk people being  people they will default back  to i'm going to ask first and if you get  hit with 100 questions a minute  you cannot answer all of them so you  need that type of initiative  we had it thank heavens  and everything is interconnected and you  have to trust them so again you have to  build those relationships back and forth  we have the people who take care of our  animals  had they fallen we would have been done  necropsy clintpath our study directors  purchasing  client services legal it goes on and on  and on and if any one of those groups  had dropped the ball  because we're interconnected we might  not have failed  although i suspect we probably would  have but it would have been immeasurably  harder  and also all of our people are dealing  with outside stress  um many of us myself included have lost  friends and loved ones during this  people at work or their  close relations could develop the virus  people could get seriously ill  people could get worse than that  there's the uncertainty have i been  exposed have i not when we couldn't get  tests  etc especially in the early days when  testing was difficult to obtain  and certainly much of my staff is  relatively young  they were not a priority for testing  back when test kits were difficult to  obtain their loved ones  have anxiety um especially when  as with all of us who have worked  through this if you're essential  personnel  then everyone around you who is not is  worried about you going out  interacting with other people what are  you opposed to  the schools are closed we had to make  numerous accommodations because  people didn't have schools or child care  for their children anymore  so flexible scheduling became very  important there were no extracurricular  activities  and people need things to do  to regather their emotional energy  all those were shut down people weren't  going to the gym they weren't going out  with friends  and so the stress just built and of  course there's an anxiety being in  public  i mean most of us can remember this as  we were driving back and forth  it was spooky being out on the roads  especially in march and april because  you were alone  and our clients are going through the  same thing everybody  that we're interacting with is going  through these same things  and so constant last-minute schedule  changes requests  you know we stayed open during this  because as our various clients and  universities  had to pull back their operations we  were the ones who were able to keep  things moving along  in all of these various areas of school  research  but all of those rippled into these  studies as well  so if i have one message in all of this  it is  take care of your people and i'll add to  it  you can't start taking care of your  people  when the disaster hits you have to have  a culture where they  know that you are looking out for them  and that you will do the right ethical  thing  for them whether you necessarily have to  or not  you have to be compassionate and yes  the structures and the expectations and  the accountability  are at least as important as they were  before because people need  structure they need to understand what  the rules and the requirements are and  if you throw all of that up  it's very difficult to work when you  don't know what the expectations are  and what we found is that having that  type of structure was actually a stress  relievers  everything is going unstructured in  people's lives  having an area where there actually is  structure  there's accountability trees and what  have you  was actually remarkably distressing it  was relaxing  in a rather odd way um we did a lot of  what i call wargaming and planning  you never make a decision as well  when everything is going sideways as you  would have when the stress is not there  and the pressure is not there and the  time constraints aren't there  and i'm very much an advocate of work  your content  work your contingency plans before  things hit the wall in my group  my supervisors came to me with a great  idea let's meet every morning at eight  o'clock let's triage do we have people  out what's the work  what are the changes that we're getting  from our clients and so on  um we ran that through may  and again this requires that dedication  and flexibility from our technicians  if i had poor technicians none of this  would have worked but i didn't  had outstanding technicians who rose to  the challenge  as an aside what was fascinating is  our efficiency and  our quality metrics  just improved dramatically over the  course of this which again  shows when people are rising to the  challenge  a whole bunch of things are just taken  in stride  but we did we were aggressive we  immediately implemented social  distancing we  broke up we have a large technician area  we broke that up we moved people around  so that we weren't sitting close  together  wherever possible we had people working  remotely we  moved our meetings to skype and team  site and we did that very very quickly  and we closed our circle we did not  bring outside visitors to sites we  didn't bring clients we didn't bring  vendors  we kept ourselves to ourselves and  whenever  and we've had uh people test positive  we have had people who didn't test  positive but required testing because of  symptoms we moved aggressively  we moved people into self quarantine we  emphasized testing we try to break  the if you will circle of people being  able to  to communicate the virus to others we  were very aggressive  and knock on wood it has stood us in  good stead to date and what this all  meant because of that flexibility  because of people willing to do things  that maybe weren't part of their  standard job  but if there's a need the study comes  first we're going to get it done and  people pitching in  our impacts have been fairly minimal  even when we've had staff  out due to either suspected infections  or actual infections  but i put this in here because we  weren't flawless not by any stretch  um and it's the little things and we  knew this beforehand  but there were things that we didn't  think of and one of the things that we  didn't think of  is we did a wonderful job preparing for  our  formal suppliers but the things that you  get at the grocery store  who would have thought that we would  have empty shelves i mean to date  whenever we've had  either an outbreak such as the avian flu  or disasters those are  fairly rapid they're not nation or  certainly worldwide  but so we uh use clear drape and we  actually use  press and seal which we ethylene oxide  sterilize  as one of our common drapes both because  it's transparent  so we can more easily monitor our  patient but it also has  a very very low tack adhesive and on  mice whose skin is very thin we don't  want to irritate it  and a lot of adhesive drapes the  adhesive is fairly aggressive because  they're designed for humans  they will cause issues for the animals  this is  much more humane and  relatively innocuous to our patients  and i'll just throw out there call it an  aside  um regardless of the species  asepsis so eliminating microorganisms  from your sterile field  analgesics ie pain control they are  important in every single species  and we treat all of our species the same  way  for this though it's present seal you  can  go and buy it at the supermarket  until you can't and all of a sudden for  several weeks  press and seal disappeared along with so  many other things from the store shelves  never considered that never occurred to  me that  we would have that type of disruption to  our commercial supply chains  we were fortunate i got some people in  my group i have a management tree we  went to all of them and said if you're  at the store and you see this stuff just  buy it  and they did and we were able to get  enough to carry us through and now that  it's back on the shelves  um we have stockpiled it so if anything  hits in the future we will be ready  and there are other examples but i put  this out there  look at your entire supply chain look at  every  single thing that you need to do your  work  what this taught me is you can't make  the assumption  consciously or unconsciously that there  are things you don't have to worry about  there are things that i didn't think we  had to worry about and we did  positive relationships with our vendors  are critical again we depended upon them  to go the extra mile  had we spent all of our time yelling at  them  had we spent all of our time every  instance  of a delay beating them down  would they have been as  frankly wonderful as they were they  might have been  but they might not have been and that's  not a risk that i'm really want to take  and certainly i'm glad that we didn't  take  and i put this in here because i think  they do deserve a call out  um our stereotax devices came from david  koff  who in the midst of a shutdown they were  still figuring out ways to get me  materials world precision instruments  uh george tamman is another one that  they were shutting down but they still  figured out a way to get us the stuff  that we needed  uh mine viewer for our pp and disposable  supplies  um there was a lot of creativity from  people  um in our animal care unit who were  taking care of that piece were jumping  through hoops to make sure that that  stuff arrived when we needed it  again had we had a bad relationship  with them beforehand we would not have  been able to reset that during all of  this  and it might have gone really really  badly for us  understanding the equipment um and  really understanding the equipment  the example that i'll use is what  happens  if the people who show up to repair your  equipment  suddenly can't can you continue  functioning  or are the things that you plug into a  wall socket  these magical boxes that someone will  come and fix  or calibrate but you don't know how they  work you don't know how to troubleshoot  them because of course you can always  call in a repair  person will be there within 24 hours  until they can't be  again you can't wait until the disaster  hits you have to be ready in advance you  have to do your due diligence and really  understand  what do we know we were fortunate  again david kopf was  that company was jumping through hoops  to get us things but there were still  delays  and our sponsor provided us some  stereotax devices  they were damaged in shipment  we had to fix them and normally what you  would do is you would send them back to  the vendor and they would repair them  but they couldn't  what is very fortunate is back in the  early 90s  i was able to attend a wonderful  three-day class on how to fix  and repair these particular pieces of  equipment and i'm kind of a nerd in that  area anyways  but it's always stuck with me how useful  it is in the middle of  for instance the study i don't have to  cancel things and wait two weeks  i can fix and repair in this case i was  able to recalibrate the units i was able  to use  wonderful laser grid um  piece of equipment to reset everything  in that enabled us to continue  if we hadn't been able to i would have  had to ship them back for refurbishment  i would still be waiting for them  because the place and the engineers that  would have fixed them  have been shut down again  it's too late to get the training when  the virus is hit you need to do that  ahead of time  and i emphasize we know that we're going  to experience  waves of effects over the next few  months  years we don't know so if you're not  ready now  there's no time like the present to  start getting that information things  are starting to loosen up a bit  now is the time to identify those gaps  and get them filled  because maybe we'll be lucky and we  won't have to go through another cycle  of this but if we do  we had best be ready  and the short version is as i said my  spoiler we were successful  um we've run we're actually over 11 but  when i wrote this we're at 11  studies with lateral ventricles  hippocampal injections and fecal  injections as well  we're now over 500 surgeries since march  we've been able to train 12 technicians  on the various support roles  in addition four surgeons apprentice  surgeons are validating they started  validating last week and they continue  today  as a matter of fact with another five to  go  we're running one or two of these  studies weekly um  we were successful and it was because of  our people  and it was because of our pre-planning  so ultimately what did i learn from this  you have to have the right people they  have to be dedicated they have to be  willing to roll with things this was a  very stressful time  and people could have been forgiven for  not being able to roll with the  metaphoric punches but they did  they needed to be trained and if we  hadn't spent the time training  and i can't take the credit for that  that's my training arm  that's my supervisors those are my team  leads who spent the time with  technicians and trained them  and then we have trained those people  they're independent  and they're empowered and we didn't  become independent and empowered when we  didn't have the bandwidth  to oversee them they were already there  before  all of this hit you've got to have the  positive functional relationships with  the various key stakeholders and  impactors it's too late to develop them  when everything is going sideways  you've got to have expertise in all of  the areas  and i could talk at great length about  all the various people who brought their  specialized experience and knowledge to  the  boot for this we had prior planning  not just the specific disaster plans but  we had redundancies and contingency  plans  just ready just in case  and we also had structures that enabled  quality from  all of our people when they were under  tremendous stress they have been  and they continue to be and stress is  debilitating over time  one of the things that my supervisors  did  is they looked for opportunities to have  appropriately social distance but events  nonetheless  to have some fun and to relieve the  stress  and to look out for people hey you know  what  why don't you work from home tomorrow  you have things coming up  again had i not had that type of an  atmosphere  and that type of a culture it would have  been too late to create it  but again my supervisors did a wonderful  job  minimizing the stress and taking care of  the people who work for them  it all comes down to you have to be  prepared you have to be ready you have  to be ready before the crisis hits  it's too bloody late to try and put all  of these things into place  after everything has gone sideways  and with that there's a picture of my  current icv  team and if you have any questions i  would be happy to answer them
 

